<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muniadona of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 995–<a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Mayor of Castile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muniadona of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> (c. 995–1066), also called Mayor, was the daughter of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-garcia-of-castile">Sancho García of Castile</a> and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>. After her brother García Sánchez, the last count, was murdered in 1029, Castile passed through her to her husband and then to their son Ferdinand, who became the first king of Castile and later king of León.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muniadona was the eldest child of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-garcia-of-castile">Sancho García of Castile</a> and his wife Urraca, who was probably a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-gomez">Banu Gómez</a> family. She was also called Mayor or Munia. She married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho Garcés III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> before 27 June 1011, when both appear confirming privileges of the Monastery of San Millán, and she was queen of Pamplona from 1011 until 1035.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her marriage gave her husband claims through her dynastic rights. In 1017 William Isarn, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, was assassinated. His county had been partitioned with his cousin Mayor García, and she and her husband Raymond III of Pallars Jussà claimed all of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho Garcés III</a> contested this, asserting the rights of Muniadona, who as eldest daughter of Mayor García's brother could claim to be heiress of her grandmother Ava de Ribagorza. His troops invaded in 1017 and took William's half.</p><p>In 1025 Mayor García, repudiated by her husband, transferred most of the rest to Sancho, and Raymond kept only the Noguera Ribagorçana basin. Muniadona may have taken the name Mayor in recognition of this. Her rights were invoked again in 1028, when the Vela family assassinated Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile">García Sánchez of Castile</a> at León. Because she was his eldest sister, Sancho III took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and their son Ferdinand Sánchez was named count in 1029. Her children were García Sánchez III, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> from 1035 to 1054; Ferdinand Sánchez, king of León; Gonzalo Sánchez, king of Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>; and Jimena Sánchez, queen of León through her marriage to Bermudo III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Muniadona outlived her husband, who died in 1035, and most of her children. As a widow she founded the monastery of San Martín de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> de Frómista, where she asked to be buried in her testament of 13 June 1066, and she probably died soon afterwards. The will freed the Saracens who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and were dependent on her, gave the monastery estates in Bobadilla and Agero and a share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> of Frómista and Población de Campos, and divided her sheep, cows and horses among the churches of Santa María, San Juan Bautista and San Martín.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muniadona of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Leon. Died in the Kingdom of Castile. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
